      "type": "academic_passage",
      "title": "The Rise of the Printing Press",
      "text": "The Rise of the Printing Press\n\nBefore the middle of the fifteenth century, books in Europe were copied by hand, usually by trained scribes working in monasteries or university workshops. A single copy of a lengthy text could occupy a scribe for months, and the resulting volume was expensive enough that most owners were institutions, nobles, or wealthy merchants. Errors accumulated as copies were made from copies, so that two manuscripts of the same work might differ in hundreds of small details.\n\nThe device that altered this situation was assembled in Mainz around 1450 by Johannes Gutenberg, a metalworker by training. His central innovation was not the press itself, which resembled machines already used for pressing grapes and paper, but movable type cast from a durable metal alloy. Each letter was produced in quantity from a mold, arranged into lines, inked, and pressed against paper. Because the type could be redistributed and reused, a printer could produce hundreds of identical copies and then set an entirely different book with the same materials.\n\nThe technology spread quickly. Within fifty years, presses were operating in more than two hundred European towns, often established by craftsmen who had trained in German workshops and then migrated south and west. The price of books fell sharply, and printers began to favor works with reliable markets: legal handbooks, grammars, devotional texts, and eventually writing in vernacular languages rather than Latin alone. Publishing became a commercial enterprise, and printers made decisions about what a reading public would buy.\n\nHistorians disagree about how far the press should be credited with the intellectual changes that followed. Some emphasize that identical copies allowed scholars in different cities to consult the same page of the same edition, which made careful comparison and correction possible. Others note that literacy rates rose slowly and that many printed works simply reproduced traditional material. What is not disputed is that the volume of text in circulation increased on a scale without precedent in European experience.",
