        "text": "How Soap Cleans\n\nWater alone is a poor cleaner of greasy surfaces. Water molecules are polar, meaning that their electrical charge is distributed unevenly, and they associate strongly with other polar substances. Oils, fats, and the grime that clings to them are nonpolar, so they neither dissolve in water nor are carried away by it. A cook who rinses a pan of bacon drippings under the tap observes the result directly: the water beads and runs off, leaving the fat behind. Soap solves this problem not by dissolving grease in the ordinary sense but by acting as a chemical intermediary between two substances that otherwise ignore each other.\n\nA soap molecule has an unusual shape. One end, the head, carries an electrical charge and is strongly attracted to water. The other end is a long chain of carbon and hydrogen atoms with no charge, and it is attracted to oils. Chemists describe such a molecule as amphiphilic, from Greek roots meaning that it loves both. This dual character is the entire basis of the cleaning action, and it arises from the manufacturing process, in which fats are heated with a strong alkali such as sodium hydroxide.\n\nWhen soap is added to water, the molecules do not remain scattered. Above a certain concentration they cluster into spheres called micelles, with the charged heads facing outward toward the water and the carbon chains tucked inward, away from it. If grease is present, it becomes trapped in the oily interior of these spheres. The exterior of each micelle is charged and therefore mixes readily with water, so the droplet of grease can be rinsed down the drain inside its chemical container.\n\nAgitation matters as well. Scrubbing and the flow of water break large deposits into smaller droplets, giving the soap more surface to act upon. Warm water helps by softening solid fats. Neither factor, however, replaces the molecular mechanism; without amphiphilic molecules, vigorous washing merely moves grease from place to place.",
